---
description: Minimal comments/docs; no chat residue or "A, not B" arguing-back prose
alwaysApply: true
---

# Comments and docs

Prefer no comment over a redundant one. When a comment or doc note is needed, make it as short as possible.

Only write about: non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints the code/docs cannot express alone.

Never write about: what the code does, parameter names, or anything immediately visible.

Applies to: inline comments, commit messages when they are explanatory asides, README/AGENTS/docs you generate or edit, rule/skill prose, and PR body text.

## No conversational residue

Never leave residue of an AI session. Usually subtle — directives like "do NOT do X here", "always call this before Y", or "ensure Z" that came from chat instructions rather than real constraints. Ask: would a developer who never saw the chat write this? If not, omit it.

```go
// BAD
// Read the body and restore it so it can be forwarded
body, _ := io.ReadAll(r.Body)
r.Body = io.NopCloser(bytes.NewReader(body))

// GOOD — one short phrase on the non-obvious constraint
// Restore before returning so a partial read never leaves the body drained.
r.Body = io.NopCloser(bytes.NewReader(body))
```

## No arguing-back style

State the chosen fact. Do not rebuff a discarded alternative as if answering an argument.

Avoid `A, not B` / `X (not Y)` when B/Y only exists because someone (or the model) considered it in chat.

```markdown
<!-- BAD — arguing back -->
Routing method: Traefik, not OpenAPI `servers`.
Use vault path (not local Docker) for day-to-day.

<!-- GOOD — state the choice -->
Routing method: Traefik.
Day-to-day: vault path (`make run`).
```

Contrast is fine only when both options are real, documented choices a reader must pick between (e.g. a comparison table of supported approaches).

## Rules, AGENTS, and other instructions

Same idea: state what to do. If a command or requirement is already explicit, do not add lines comparing it to alternatives or justifying the choice—add prose only when it prevents a likely mistake.
